-- title:
--   Bounded denominators for functions with poles only at ∞̄
-- statement:
--   Let $p$ be a prime and $N\ge 1$ a natural number, and let $g$ belong to `modularFunctionFieldFull N`, the subfield of the Laurent series field $\mathbb{Q}(\!(q)\!)$ generated over $\mathbb{Q}$ by the series $\mathrm{qExpand}\,\mathbb{Q}\,d\,j_q$ for the nonzero divisors $d$ of $N$. Write $\bar g$ for the image of $g$ under `coeffEmb`, the coefficientwise map $\mathbb{Q}(\!(q)\!)\to\overline{\mathbb{Q}}(\!(q)\!)$ induced by $\mathbb{Q}\hookrightarrow\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$, viewed as an element of `modularFunctionFieldBar N`, the intermediate field of $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}(\!(q)\!)$ over $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ generated by the image of `modularFunctionFieldFull N`. Assume that for every place $W$ of this field over $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ — that is, every valuation subring containing $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$, distinct from the whole field and a principal ideal ring — other than the $q$-adic cusp place `cuspInftyBar N`, one has $\mathrm{ord}_W(\bar g)\ge 0$, where $\mathrm{ord}_W$ is minus the logarithm of the associated $\mathbb{Z}^{m0}$-valued adic valuation. Then there is a natural number $M$ such that $0\le v_p\bigl(p^{M}\,a_m\bigr)$ for every $m\in\mathbb{Z}$, where $a_m$ denotes the $m$-th Laurent coefficient of $g$ and $v_p$ the $p$-adic valuation on $\mathbb{Q}$.
--
--   This is the $\bar\infty$-half of the bounded denominators statement for rational functions on $X_0(N)$: a function whose only pole is at the cusp $\bar\infty$ has $q$-expansion coefficients with denominators bounded by a single power of $p$. It supplies the $p$-integrality input for the construction of the families of functions used in the multiplicative covering step, being cited by [`ModularCurve.MultCovering.exists_famCtx`](thm.html#ModularCurve.MultCovering.exists_famCtx) and [`ModularCurve.MultCovering.exists_famCtx_orth_linearIndependent_zeroChart_residue`](thm.html#ModularCurve.MultCovering.exists_famCtx_orth_linearIndependent_zeroChart_residue).
